{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed relation is 'abstraction,' but the target concedes its own refutation condition mid-artifact: it admits SRC_003's routing-rot rank could 'depend on call-graph frequency rather than blast-radius category.' If the shared schema is genuinely inherited by all six artifacts, priority must be derivable from blast-radius alone; yet the target acknowledges an alternative causal determinant (call-graph frequency) that is independent of blast radius. This is not a harmless caveat — it is an admission that the abstraction leaks: at least one artifact's priority may be governed by a frequency-of-occurrence mechanism rather than by the five-tuple blast-radius schema, meaning the claimed 'single shared ranking schema' does not actually unify the artifacts. The relation is therefore not substantively an inheritance/abstraction; it is a disjunctive classification that fails the moment the conceded alternative materializes.","counterexample":["SRC_003 is assigned its priority on the basis of call-graph frequency (how many callers hit the routing-rot path) rather than on the blast-radius tuple (corrupts-record, bypasses-security, breaks-routing, disables-recovery, observability-gap) — the abstraction's own refutation trigger fires."],"target":"0fba41d03397705ca2086d66608168f6cb3ef1c5438d1ee0faad0712fa279f41"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claimed 'shared mechanism' is a false equivalence. The census-delta case (verify_root_report converting ERROR to verdict) is genuinely a reclassification: an error-class signal is re-labelled as a non-error verdict while the underlying event is preserved. But the recovery guard's behavior — converting a context-receipt error into a silent proceed — is not a classification act; it is a control-flow decision to ignore the error and continue execution. Classification produces a label; fallback/transition produces a control outcome. The target's own refutation clause distinguishes 'error reclassification' from 'state transition or fallback,' yet the recovery guard's 'silent proceed' is paradigmatically a fallback (continue despite error), not a re-labelling of the error's class. The shared label 'silent-classification-drift' is therefore imposed on two mechanisms whose causal structure differs: one transforms the signal's type, the other suppresses the signal's effect.","counterexample":["Recovery guard checks context-receipt, sees an error, and chooses 'proceed' (a control-flow transition / fallback path) while leaving the error's class unchanged as an error internally — the error is never reclassified to a non-error-class event, it is merely ignored."],"target":"320c39ab6c25fd527dd357e12889b665542387dd057d0c2aeca001a70dd339ff"},{"attack":true,"case":"The dependence claim is self-refuting under the target's own counterexample. The target asserts SRC_004 *depends on* SRC_005 because the test requires prior design knowledge, but then provides a concrete refutation: a parameterised expectation (assert ERROR OR assert verdict, document which) makes the test self-contained. That parameterised test is not hypothetical — it is a standard, implementable pattern (conditional assertion with documented branch). Because the target itself names a viable test design that needs no prior investigation, the 'sequential dependence' relation is falsified by the target's own content. The relation is not substantive: it collapses from 'gates' to 'is one option for,' which is not a dependence relation.","counterexample":["The test is written as: if design says 'by bug' then assert ERROR; if design says 'by design' then assert verdict; either branch is documented and runnable — no prior SRC_005 investigation result is required to author or run the test."],"target":"d91a203752f1cb656ff0e9023df84fcf93af051c4db29077f81f7ce2386c9cba"}]}